{
  "sub_unit_name": "Deletion Operations in Linked Lists",
  "subject_short_code": "DSA100",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "What is Deletion?",
      "description": "Deletion is the process of removing a node from a Linked List and reconnecting the remaining nodes correctly."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Deletion is one of the most important Linked List operations. Unlike arrays, Linked Lists can remove elements without shifting data. The challenge lies in correctly updating pointers so that the list remains connected.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Key Advantage",
      "content": "Linked Lists can delete nodes efficiently without moving other elements."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Why Deletion Matters"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Many real-world systems frequently remove data such as completed tasks, closed browser tabs, processed requests, or deleted records. Linked Lists make these operations efficient.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "comparison-card",
      "left_title": "Array Deletion",
      "right_title": "Linked List Deletion",
      "left_points": [
        "Requires shifting",
        "O(n) complexity",
        "Fixed memory structure",
        "Less flexible"
      ],
      "right_points": [
        "No shifting required",
        "Pointer updates only",
        "Dynamic structure",
        "Efficient modifications"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Types of Deletion Operations"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Common Deletions",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Delete from Beginning",
          "answer": "Remove the Head node."
        },
        {
          "question": "Delete from End",
          "answer": "Remove the Tail node."
        },
        {
          "question": "Delete at Specific Position",
          "answer": "Remove a node located at a given index."
        },
        {
          "question": "Delete by Value",
          "answer": "Find and remove a node containing a target value."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deletion from Beginning"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Deleting the first node is the simplest deletion operation because only the Head pointer needs to be updated.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Before Deletion",
      "steps": ["Head", " ↓ ", "[10] → [20] → [30] → [40]"]
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "After Deleting 10",
      "steps": ["Head", " ↓ ", "[20] → [30] → [40]"]
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "Delete Beginning Steps",
      "steps": [
        "Store Head Node",
        "Move Head to Next Node",
        "Delete Old Head",
        "Operation Complete"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Pointer Update",
      "description": "Head = Head.next"
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Delete Beginning",
      "time_complexity": "O(1)",
      "space_complexity": "O(1)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deletion from End"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Deleting the last node requires locating the second-last node and updating its next pointer to NULL.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Before End Deletion",
      "steps": ["[10] → [20] → [30] → [40] → NULL"]
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "After Deleting 40",
      "steps": ["[10] → [20] → [30] → NULL"]
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "Delete End Steps",
      "steps": [
        "Traverse to Second Last Node",
        "Store Last Node",
        "Set Next = NULL",
        "Delete Last Node"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "warning-box",
      "title": "Important Observation",
      "content": "Without a Tail pointer, deletion at the end requires traversal."
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Delete End",
      "time_complexity": "O(n)",
      "space_complexity": "O(1)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deletion at a Specific Position"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Sometimes we need to delete a node at a specific index inside the Linked List.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Delete Position 3",
      "steps": ["[10] → [20] → [30] → [40]", "Remove 30", "[10] → [20] → [40]"]
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "Delete Position Steps",
      "steps": [
        "Find Previous Node",
        "Store Target Node",
        "Skip Target Node",
        "Delete Target Node"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Pointer Adjustment",
      "description": "Previous.next = Target.next"
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Delete at Position",
      "time_complexity": "O(n)",
      "space_complexity": "O(1)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deletion by Value"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Deletion by value requires searching for a node containing a specific value before removing it.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Delete Value 20",
      "steps": ["[10] → [20] → [30]", "Find 20", "[10] → [30]"]
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "Delete by Value Steps",
      "steps": [
        "Search Node",
        "Find Previous Node",
        "Update Links",
        "Delete Node"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Delete by Value",
      "time_complexity": "O(n)",
      "space_complexity": "O(1)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deleting a Known Node"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>If the target node and its previous node are already known, deletion becomes very efficient.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Known Node Deletion",
      "steps": ["Prev → Target → Next", "Prev skips Target", "Prev → Next"]
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Efficiency",
      "content": "When node references are already available, deletion can often be completed in O(1) time."
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Known Node Deletion",
      "time_complexity": "O(1)",
      "space_complexity": "O(1)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deleting from an Empty List"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Before deleting, always verify that the Linked List contains at least one node.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Empty List",
      "steps": ["Head = NULL", "Deletion Requested", "Operation Not Possible"]
    },

    {
      "type": "warning-box",
      "title": "Edge Case",
      "content": "Attempting deletion on an empty list can cause runtime errors if NULL checks are not performed."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deleting the Only Node"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>If a Linked List contains only one node, deleting it makes the list empty.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Single Node Deletion",
      "steps": ["Head → [10]", "Delete 10", "Head = NULL"]
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Special Case",
      "content": "After deletion, both Head and Tail become NULL."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Pointer Visualization"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Node Removal Process",
      "steps": [
        "Prev → Target → Next",
        "Update Prev.next",
        "Skip Target",
        "Delete Target"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Golden Rule",
      "description": "Always reconnect neighboring nodes before deleting the target node."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Common Deletion Mistakes"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Mistakes to Avoid",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Losing References",
          "answer": "Deleting a node before reconnecting links can break the list."
        },
        {
          "question": "Missing NULL Checks",
          "answer": "Always verify nodes exist before accessing pointers."
        },
        {
          "question": "Incorrect Traversal",
          "answer": "Wrong position calculations may delete unintended nodes."
        },
        {
          "question": "Not Updating Head",
          "answer": "Beginning deletions require Head modification."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Real-World Applications"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Where Deletion is Used",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Task Managers",
          "answer": "Remove completed tasks."
        },
        {
          "question": "Browser Tabs",
          "answer": "Close unwanted tabs."
        },
        {
          "question": "Playlists",
          "answer": "Delete songs dynamically."
        },
        {
          "question": "Operating Systems",
          "answer": "Remove terminated processes."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Complexity Analysis"
    },

    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": ["Deletion Type", "Time Complexity"],
      "rows": [
        ["Beginning", "O(1)"],
        ["End", "O(n)"],
        ["Specific Position", "O(n)"],
        ["Delete by Value", "O(n)"],
        ["Known Node", "O(1)"]
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "comparison-card",
      "left_title": "Best Case",
      "right_title": "Worst Case",
      "left_points": [
        "Beginning Deletion",
        "Known Node",
        "Direct Access",
        "O(1)"
      ],
      "right_points": [
        "Need Traversal",
        "Delete End",
        "Search Required",
        "O(n)"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deletion vs Insertion"
    },

    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": ["Operation", "Beginning", "End", "Position"],
      "rows": [
        ["Insertion", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)"],
        ["Deletion", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)"]
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "warning-box",
      "title": "Interview Tip",
      "content": "In coding interviews, always explain which pointers are updated and why. Interviewers often focus more on pointer handling than the actual code."
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Interview Insight",
      "description": "Deletion problems are designed to test pointer manipulation, edge-case handling, and memory management concepts."
    },

    {
      "type": "revision-card",
      "title": "Quick Revision",
      "points": [
        "Deletion removes a node from the list.",
        "Beginning deletion takes O(1).",
        "End deletion usually takes O(n).",
        "Position deletion requires traversal.",
        "Delete-by-value requires searching.",
        "Known-node deletion can be O(1).",
        "Always reconnect nodes first.",
        "Handle empty lists carefully.",
        "Update Head when deleting the first node.",
        "Pointer management is the key concept."
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "quiz",
      "question": "Which deletion operation always takes O(1)?",
      "options": [
        "Delete Beginning",
        "Delete End",
        "Delete by Value",
        "Delete Position"
      ],
      "answer": "Delete Beginning"
    },

    {
      "type": "quiz",
      "question": "What should happen after deleting the only node in a list?",
      "options": [
        "Head points to itself",
        "Head becomes NULL",
        "Node remains",
        "Tail stores data"
      ],
      "answer": "Head becomes NULL"
    },

    {
      "type": "quiz",
      "question": "What is the most important step during deletion?",
      "options": [
        "Sorting nodes",
        "Reconnecting pointers",
        "Reversing list",
        "Creating new nodes"
      ],
      "answer": "Reconnecting pointers"
    }
  ]
}
